const mencius02_liangHuiWangXia = [
  {
    id: 8,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.1",
    title: { zh: "與民同樂", en: "Share joy with the people" },
    text: {
      zh: `莊暴見孟子，曰：「暴見於王，王語暴以好樂，暴未有以對也。」曰：「好樂何如？」 

　　孟子曰：「王之好樂甚，則齊國其庶幾乎!」 

　　他日見於王，曰：「王嘗語莊子以好樂，有諸？」 

　　王變乎色，曰：「寡人非能好先王之樂也，直好世俗之樂耳。」 

　　曰：「王之好樂甚，則齊其庶幾乎！今之樂，猶古之樂也。」 

　　曰：「可得聞與？」 

　　曰：「獨樂樂，與人樂樂，孰樂？」 

　　曰：「不若與人。」 

　　曰：「與少樂樂，與眾樂樂，孰樂？」 

　　曰：「不若與眾。」 

　　「臣請為王言樂。今王鼓樂於此，百姓聞王鐘鼓之聲，管龠之音，舉疾首蹙頞而相告曰：『吾王之好鼓樂，夫何使我至於此極也？父子不相見，兄弟妻子離散。』今王田獵於此，百姓聞王車馬之音，見羽旄之美，舉疾首蹙頞而相告曰：『吾王之好田獵，夫何使我至於此極也？父子不相見，兄弟妻子離散。』此無他，不與民同樂也。今王鼓樂於此，百姓聞王鐘鼓之聲，管龠之音，舉欣欣然有喜色而相告曰：『吾王庶幾無疾病與，何以能鼓樂也？』今王田獵於此，百姓聞王車馬之音，見羽旄之美，舉欣欣然有喜色而相告曰：『吾王庶幾無疾病與，何以能田獵也？』此無他，與民同樂也。今王與百姓同樂，則王矣。」`,
      en: `Zhuang Bao saw Mencius and said, "I had an audience with the king, and the king spoke to me of his love of music; I had nothing with which to reply." He added, "What do you think of loving music?"

Mencius said, "If the king's love of music were deep, then the state of Qi would be near success!"

On another day, seeing the king, Mencius said, "Your Majesty once spoke to Zhuangzi of loving music — was that so?"

The king's face changed, and he said, "I am not able to love the music of the former kings; I merely love the music of the present age."

Mencius said, "If Your Majesty's love of music were deep, then Qi would be near success! Today's music is like the music of old."

"May I hear of this?"

"Which is more enjoyable — to enjoy music alone, or to enjoy it with others?"

"Not as good as with others."

"Which is more enjoyable — to enjoy music with a few, or with the many?"

"Not as good as with the many."

"Let me speak to Your Majesty of music. Suppose now Your Majesty makes music here, and the people, hearing the sound of your bells and drums, pipes and flutes, all with aching heads and knitted brows say to one another, 'Our king's love of music — why does he bring us to this extreme? Fathers and sons do not see one another; brothers, wives, and children are scattered.' Suppose Your Majesty hunts here, and the people, hearing the sound of your carriages and horses and seeing the beauty of the feathered pennons, all with aching heads and knitted brows say to one another, 'Our king's love of hunting — why does he bring us to this extreme? Fathers and sons do not see one another; brothers, wives, and children are scattered.' This is for no other reason than that you do not share joy with the people. Now suppose Your Majesty makes music here, and the people, hearing your bells and drums, pipes and flutes, all with glad faces say to one another, 'Our king is hopefully free of illness — how else could he make music?' Suppose you hunt here, and they, hearing carriages and horses and seeing the pennons, all with glad faces say, 'Our king is hopefully free of illness — how else could he hunt?' This is for no other reason than that you share joy with the people. If now Your Majesty shares joy with the hundred surnames, you will be a true king."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `莊暴見孟子，說自己朝見齊宣王時，王親口談到愛好音樂，自己卻答不上話，便向孟子請教「好樂」該如何看待。孟子先給出一個伏筆：如果宣王真的深深愛好音樂，齊國恐怕就有希望了！改天孟子見到宣王，直接問起這件事；宣王面色一變，坦承自己並非能愛好先王的雅樂，只不過喜歡世俗流行的音樂罷了。孟子不但不介意，反而說：只要大王愛好音樂到極深的地步，齊國就有希望——今天的俗樂與古代的雅樂，道理其實相通。宣王請他細說，孟子便用一連串反問逼近核心：獨自欣賞音樂快樂，還是與別人一起欣賞快樂？宣王答不如與人同樂。與少數人一起快樂，還是與眾人一起快樂？宣王答不如與眾同樂。孟子於是為宣王細說「樂」的兩種對照畫面：假如大王在這裡奏樂，百姓聽見鐘鼓管龠之聲，個個愁眉苦臉地互相抱怨「我們的王這麼愛奏樂，怎麼把我們害到這般地步？父子不能相見，兄弟妻兒離散」；假如大王在這裡田獵，百姓聽見車馬之音、看見羽旄之美，同樣疾首蹙額地抱怨「我們的王這麼愛打獵，怎麼把我們害成這樣」——這沒有別的緣故，只是因為不與民同樂。反過來，假如大王奏樂或田獵時，百姓聽見同樣的聲音，卻個個喜形於色，互相慶幸「我們的王大概沒有疾病吧，不然怎麼還能奏樂、田獵？」——這也沒有別的緣故，只是因為與民同樂。兩種場面的差別，全在於是否讓百姓一起分享快樂。孟子最後總結：如今大王若能與百姓同樂，就可以稱王天下了。孟子整段對話的重心，不在音樂雅俗，而在「同樂」二字：獨樂不如與人，與少不如與眾；百姓疾首蹙額還是欣欣然有喜色，全看君王是否與民同樂。田獵、鼓樂本身不必禁絕，若百姓因此父子離散、兄弟妻子分離，那便是不與民同樂；若百姓反為君王無病能樂而喜，那便是王道將成的氣象。`,
      en: `Zhuang Bao tells Mencius that when he had an audience with the king, the king spoke of his love of music, and Zhuang Bao had no answer ready — so he asks what to make of "loving music." Mencius plants a hint: if the king's love of music runs deep, the state of Qi may be near success! On another day, seeing the king, Mencius raises it directly; the king's face changes, and he admits he cannot love the music of the former kings — he merely loves the popular music of the age. Far from minding, Mencius says that if the love runs deep, Qi will still be near success, and that today's music is like the music of old. Invited to explain, Mencius closes in with questions: which is more enjoyable — to enjoy music alone, or with others? The king admits with others is better. With a few, or with the many? With the many is better. Mencius then paints two contrasting scenes of "music." Suppose the king makes music here, and the people, hearing bells, drums, pipes, and flutes, all with aching heads and knitted brows say to one another, "Our king's love of music — why does he bring us to this extreme? Fathers and sons do not see one another; brothers, wives, and children are scattered." Suppose he hunts here, and they, hearing carriages and horses and seeing the feathered pennons, complain the same way about his love of hunting — this is for no other reason than that he does not share joy with the people. Now suppose instead that when he makes music or hunts, the people hear the same sounds and all with glad faces say, "Our king is hopefully free of illness — how else could he make music or hunt?" — this too is for no other reason than that he shares joy with the people. The entire difference between the two scenes lies in whether the people are allowed to share the pleasure. Mencius concludes: if now Your Majesty shares joy with the hundred surnames, you will be a true king. The whole dialogue turns not on whether music is refined or popular, but on "sharing joy": alone is less than with others, few less than many; whether the people knit their brows or brighten with glad faces depends entirely on whether the king shares joy with them. Music and hunting need not be banned; if they scatter fathers and sons and break up families, that is not sharing joy; if the people instead rejoice that their king is well enough to make music, that is the atmosphere of approaching true kingship.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `個人嗜好本身未必錯，錯在讓別人為你的快樂買單。享樂前先問：旁人是一起高興，還是暗自受苦？
<br /><b>建議：今天進行一項娛樂或慶祝前，先確認最受影響的一兩個人是否也被照顧到，必要時調整規模或時間。</b>`,
      en: `A personal taste is not wrong in itself; wrong is making others pay for your pleasure. Before enjoying, ask whether others rejoice with you or quietly suffer.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Before one entertainment or celebration today, check that the one or two people most affected are also cared for; adjust scale or timing if needed.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 9,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.2",
    title: { zh: "囿與民同", en: "A park shared with the people" },
    text: {
      zh: `齊宣王問曰：「文王之囿方七十里，有諸？」 

　　孟子對曰：「於傳有之。」 

　　曰：「若是其大乎？」 

　　曰：「民猶以為小也。」 

　　曰：「寡人之囿方四十里，民猶以為大，何也？」 

　　曰：「文王之囿方七十里，芻蕘者往焉，雉兔者往焉，與民同之。民以為小，不亦宜乎？臣始至於境，問國之大禁，然後敢入。臣聞郊關之內有囿方四十里，殺其麋鹿者如殺人之罪，則是方四十里為阱於國中。民以為大，不亦宜乎？」`,
      en: `King Xuan of Qi asked, "Was King Wen's park seventy li square — is that so?"

Mencius replied, "It is so in the records."

"Was it so large?"

"The people still thought it small."

"My park is forty li square, and the people still think it large — why?"

"King Wen's park was seventy li square; gatherers of fuel and grass went there, and hunters of pheasants and hares went there — he shared it with the people. That they thought it small was fitting, was it not? When I first reached the border, I asked about the great prohibitions of the state, and only then dared enter. I heard that within the suburban passes there is a park forty li square, and that to kill its deer is like the crime of killing a person — so that forty li square is a trap set in the midst of the state. That the people think it large is fitting, is it not?"`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `齊宣王聽說文王的獵園方圓七十里，覺得大得驚人，便問孟子是否屬實。孟子答：確有記載，而且百姓還嫌它小呢。宣王詫異：這麼大還嫌小？孟子解釋：文王的獵園雖有七十里，卻對百姓開放——打柴的人可以進去打柴，獵雉兔的人可以進去獵雉兔，與民共用共享，百姓自然不會覺得它妨礙自己，反倒嫌它不夠大。宣王這才說出實情：自己的獵園只有四十里，百姓卻嫌它太大，這是為什麼呢？孟子直指問題核心：他初到齊國邊境時，先打聽當地的重大禁令才敢入境，聽說國都近郊有一座方圓四十里的獵園，若殺了裡面的麋鹿，其罪等同殺人——這等於是在國境之中挖了一個方圓四十里的陷阱。百姓進不去、用不到，還要時時提防誤觸重罪，當然會覺得它太大、太具威脅性。同樣是獵園，一個因與民共享而顯得小，一個因獨佔禁民而顯得大——差別全在於君主是否把資源真正與百姓同享。`,
      en: `King Xuan of Qi, having heard that King Wen's hunting park was seventy li square, finds this astonishingly large and asks Mencius whether it is true. Mencius confirms it is recorded so, and adds that the people even thought it too small. Surprised, the king asks how something so large could seem small. Mencius explains: King Wen's park, though seventy li, was open to the people — woodcutters could gather fuel there, hunters could pursue pheasants and hares there, shared freely with everyone, so naturally the people never felt it stood in their way, and thought it not large enough. Only then does the king reveal his own situation: his park is merely forty li, yet the people call it too large — why? Mencius goes straight to the heart of the matter: when he first arrived at the Qi border, he asked about the state's major prohibitions before daring to enter, and learned that near the capital there is a forty-li park where killing a deer carries the same penalty as killing a person — in effect, a forty-li trap dug into the middle of the state. The people cannot use it and must constantly guard against triggering a capital offense, so of course they find it oppressively large. The same kind of park appears small when shared with the people and large when hoarded and forbidden to them — the entire difference lies in whether the ruler genuinely shares the resource with his people.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `資源看起來「夠大」卻設禁令排他，反而令人感到壓迫；開放共用，即使規模大也被接受。
<br /><b>建議：檢查一項你掌控的空間或資源（會議室、工具、資訊），去掉一條不必要的排他規則，讓該用的人更能用到。</b>`,
      en: `A resource that looks 'big enough' but is fenced off feels oppressive; shared access makes even large holdings acceptable.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Review one space or resource you control (room, tools, information) and drop one needless exclusion rule so those who need it can use it.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 10,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.3",
    title: { zh: "大勇安民", en: "Great courage that settles the people" },
    text: {
      zh: `齊宣王問曰：「交鄰國有道乎？」 

　　孟子對曰：「有。惟仁者為能以大事小，是故湯事葛，文王事昆夷。惟智者為能以小事大，故太王事獯鬻，勾踐事吳。以大事小者，樂天者也；以小事大者，畏天者也。樂天者，保天下；畏天者，保其國。《詩》云：『畏天之威，於時保之。』」 

　　王曰：「大哉言矣！寡人有疾，寡人好勇。」 

　　對曰：「王請無好小勇。夫撫劍疾視曰：『彼惡敢當我哉！』此匹夫之勇，敵一人者也。王請大之！《詩》云：『王赫斯怒，爰整其旅，以遏徂莒，以篤周祜，以對於天下。』此文王之勇也。文王一怒而安天下之民。《書》曰：『天降下民，作之君，作之師，惟曰：其助上帝，寵之四方。有罪無罪，惟我在，天下曷敢有越厥志？』一人衡行於天下，武王恥之，此武王之勇也。而武王亦一怒而安天下之民。今王亦一怒而安天下之民，民惟恐王之不好勇也。」`,
      en: `King Xuan of Qi asked, "Is there a way to deal with neighboring states?"

Mencius replied, "There is. Only the humane can serve the small with the large — thus Tang served Ge, and King Wen served the Kunyi. Only the wise can serve the large with the small — thus King Tai served the Xunyu, and Goujian served Wu. To serve the small with the large is to delight in Heaven; to serve the large with the small is to stand in awe of Heaven. Those who delight in Heaven protect the world; those who stand in awe of Heaven protect their state. The Poetry says, 'Stand in awe of Heaven's majesty, and thereby protect it.'"

The king said, "Great words indeed! I have a fault: I love courage."

Mencius replied, "I beg Your Majesty not to love petty courage. To stroke one's sword, glare, and say, 'How dare he face me!' — this is the courage of a common fellow, matching one person. I beg Your Majesty to make it great! The Poetry says, 'The king blazed in anger; then he marshaled his troops, to stop those marching on Ju, to make firm Zhou's blessing, to answer throughout the world.' This was King Wen's courage. In one anger King Wen settled the people of the world. The Documents say, 'Heaven sent down the people and made for them rulers and teachers, saying only: Help God, favor them in the four quarters. Whether guilty or not, it rests with me alone — who under Heaven dares cross his will?' When one man ran amok under Heaven, King Wu was ashamed of it — this was King Wu's courage. And King Wu also in one anger settled the people of the world. If now Your Majesty also in one anger settles the people of the world, the people will only fear that Your Majesty does not love courage."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `齊宣王問與鄰國相處是否有一定之道。孟子答：有。只有真正仁者才能以強大之國事奉弱小之國，商湯事奉葛國、文王事奉昆夷便是如此；只有真正智者才能以弱小之國事奉強大之國，太王事奉獯鬻、勾踐事奉吳國便是如此。以大事小的人，是樂天者，能保有整個天下；以小事大的人，是畏天者，能保有自己的國家。《詩經》說：「畏天之威，於時保之」，正是這個道理。宣王聽了讚歎「這話說得真好」，卻話鋒一轉，坦承自己有個毛病——喜好勇武。孟子並不否定「好勇」本身，而是提醒他分辨勇的層次：那種按劍怒目、喝道「他豈敢與我對抗」的勇，不過是匹夫之勇，只能對付一個人，格局太小。他請宣王把這股好勇之心放大：《詩經》記載文王一發怒，便整頓軍隊、阻止敵軍侵犯莒地、鞏固周朝的福祉、向天下昭示正義，這便是文王之勇——他一怒便安定了天下百姓。《尚書》又說，上天降生百姓，為他們立君、立師，正是要幫助上帝、施恩於四方；有罪無罪，全在於我，天下人豈敢違背這樣的心志？武王便是以此為恥，見有人橫行天下便挺身而出，這便是武王之勇——他同樣一怒便安定了天下百姓。孟子最後直指宣王：如今大王若也能一怒而安定天下百姓，百姓只怕還嫌大王不夠好勇呢！`,
      en: `King Xuan of Qi asks whether there is a proper way to deal with neighboring states. Mencius answers: there is. Only a truly humane ruler can serve the small with the strength of the great — as Tang served Ge, and King Wen served the Kunyi; only a truly wise ruler can serve the great with the weakness of the small — as King Tai served the Xunyu, and Goujian served Wu. Serving the small from strength is delighting in Heaven, and preserves the whole world; serving the great from weakness is standing in awe of Heaven, and preserves one's own state. The Book of Poetry says, "Stand in awe of Heaven's majesty, and thereby preserve it" — exactly this principle. The king praises the words as excellent, then confesses a fault of his own: he loves courage. Mencius does not reject the love of courage itself but urges the king to distinguish its levels: the kind that grips a sword, glares, and snarls "How dare he stand against me!" is merely a common fellow's courage, good for facing down one man — far too small in scope. He asks the king to enlarge this impulse: the Poetry records that when King Wen once blazed with anger, he marshaled his troops, halted an army marching on Ju, secured the blessings of Zhou, and proclaimed justice to the whole world — this was King Wen's courage, and with a single burst of anger he settled the people of the entire realm. The Documents likewise say that Heaven, in sending down the people, established rulers and teachers for them precisely so they might assist the Lord on High and extend favor to all four quarters; guilt or innocence rests with him alone, and who under Heaven would dare defy such resolve? King Wu, ashamed to see one man running riot over the world, rose to the challenge in just this spirit — this was King Wu's courage, and he too settled the people of the realm with a single burst of anger. Mencius closes by turning directly to the king: if Your Majesty, too, can settle the people of the realm with one burst of anger, the people will only worry that Your Majesty does not love courage enough!`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `勇氣若只用來贏個人面子，格局很小；用來保護弱者、制止橫行，才是大勇。
<br /><b>建議：今天遇到一次想「硬碰硬」的衝動時，先問：這一怒是為安人，還是為逞強？選前者的具體行動做一次。</b>`,
      en: `Courage used only to win face is small; courage that protects the weak and stops the lawless is great.
<br /><b>Suggestion: When you feel an urge to clash head-on today, ask whether the anger is to settle people or to show force — then take one concrete action of the former kind.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 11,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.4",
    title: { zh: "樂以天下", en: "Joy and sorrow with the world" },
    text: {
      zh: `齊宣王見孟子於雪宮。王曰：「賢者亦有此樂乎？」 

　　孟子對曰：「有。人不得，則非其上矣。不得而非其上者，非也；為民上，而不與民同樂者，亦非也。樂民之樂者，民亦樂其樂；憂民之憂者，民亦憂其憂。樂以天下，憂以天下；然而不王者，未之有也。昔者齊景公問於晏子曰：『吾欲觀於轉附、朝舞，遵海而南，放於琅邪；吾何修而可以比於先王觀也？』晏子對曰：『善哉問也！天子適諸侯曰巡狩。巡狩者，巡所守也。諸侯朝於天子曰述職。述職者，述所職也。無非事者。春省耕而補不足，秋省斂而助不給。夏諺曰：『吾王不游，吾何以休？吾王不豫，吾何以助？一遊一豫，為諸侯度。』今也不然：師行而糧食，饑者弗食，勞者弗息；睊睊胥讒，民乃作慝。方命虐民，飲食若流；流連荒亡，為諸侯憂。從流下而忘反，謂之流；從流上而忘反，謂之連；從獸無厭，謂之荒；樂酒無厭，謂之亡。先王無流連之樂，荒亡之行，惟君所行也。』景公說，大戒於國，出捨於郊。於是始興發補不足。召大師曰：『為我作君臣相說之樂。』蓋徴招、角招是也。其詩曰：『畜君何尤？』畜君者，好君也。」`,
      en: `King Xuan of Qi received Mencius in the Snow Palace. The king said, "Do the worthy also have such pleasures?"

Mencius replied, "They do. When people cannot obtain them, they blame their superiors. To fail to obtain and then blame superiors is wrong; to be over the people and not share joy with them is also wrong. One who delights in the people's delight, the people also delight in his delight; one who sorrows in the people's sorrow, the people also sorrow in his sorrow. To delight with the world and sorrow with the world — and yet not become a true king — such a case has never been. Formerly Duke Jing of Qi asked Yanzi, 'I wish to view Zhuanfu and Chaowu, follow the sea south, and go out to Langye; what must I cultivate to compare with the viewing tours of the former kings?' Yanzi replied, 'An excellent question! When the Son of Heaven visits the feudal lords it is called a tour of inspection — inspecting what is guarded. When the lords appear at court before the Son of Heaven it is called reporting on office — reporting on their duties. None of it is without business. In spring one inspects the plowing and supplies what is lacking; in autumn one inspects the harvest and aids those who fall short. A Xia proverb says: "If our king does not travel, how shall we rest? If our king does not enjoy, how shall we be aided? One tour, one enjoyment — a standard for the lords." Now it is not so: armies march and consume grain; the hungry do not eat, the toiling do not rest; eyes glare and slanders rise, and the people turn to wickedness. Orders are opposed and the people oppressed; food and drink flow like a stream; flowing, lingering, wildness, and ruin become a worry for the lords. To follow the current down and forget to return is called flowing; to follow it up and forget to return is called lingering; to chase beasts without satiety is wildness; to love wine without satiety is ruin. The former kings had no pleasures of flowing and lingering, no conduct of wildness and ruin. It is only what the ruler chooses to do.' Duke Jing was pleased, issued great warnings in the state, and went out to lodge in the suburbs. Then he began to open stores and supply what was lacking. He summoned the grand music master and said, 'Compose for me music of ruler and minister delighting in each other.' These were presumably the Zhi Shao and Jue Shao. Their lyric says, 'What fault is there in cherishing the ruler?' — to cherish the ruler is to love the ruler."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `齊宣王在雪宮接見孟子，問道：賢者也會有這樣的享樂嗎？孟子回答：有的。百姓若得不到這樣的享受，便會怨責在上位者；然而百姓因得不到而怨責上位者固然不對，身為百姓的君長卻不肯與百姓一同享樂，同樣也不對。以百姓的快樂為快樂的君主，百姓也會以他的快樂為快樂；以百姓的憂愁為憂愁的君主，百姓也會以他的憂愁為憂愁。能以天下人之樂為樂、以天下人之憂為憂，卻不能成就王業的，從古至今未曾有過。孟子接著講了一個故事：從前齊景公問晏子，自己想效法先王巡遊轉附、朝舞等地，沿海南下直到琅邪，該如何修養德行才能與先王的巡遊相提並論？晏子回答得妙：天子巡視諸侯叫做「巡狩」，意思是巡視自己所守護的疆土；諸侯朝見天子叫做「述職」，意思是報告自己所盡的職責——沒有一項不是正事。春天視察耕種、補助不足的人家，秋天視察收成、接濟匱乏的人家。夏朝的諺語說：「我王不出遊，我們怎能休息？我王不巡視，我們怎能得到幫助？」君王的一遊一豫，本該是諸侯效法的榜樣。然而如今卻不是這樣：軍隊出巡耗費糧食，飢餓的人吃不上飯，勞累的人得不到休息，百姓側目而視、互相怨謗，於是滋生邪惡之心；君王違背天命、虐待百姓、飲食揮霍如流水，沉溺於「流、連、荒、亡」之中，成為諸侯的憂患。順流而下忘記回頭叫做「流」，逆流而上忘記回頭叫做「連」，追逐禽獸永不滿足叫做「荒」，沉溺酒色永不滿足叫做「亡」。先王從沒有流連的享樂、荒亡的行徑，一切全看君王自己怎麼選擇。景公聽了十分高興，在國內大加告誡，自己也搬到郊外居住，並開始開倉賑濟不足的百姓，還召見太師說：「替我譜寫一首君臣同樂的樂曲。」這便是後來的〈徵招〉〈角招〉，歌詞裡唱道：「畜君何尤？」——「畜君」就是愛戴君主的意思。`,
      en: `King Xuan of Qi receives Mencius in the Snow Palace and asks whether even the worthy enjoy such pleasures. Mencius answers that they do; yet if the people cannot obtain such pleasures, they resent their ruler — and while resenting a ruler out of want is itself wrong, a ruler over the people who will not share pleasure with them is equally wrong. A ruler who delights in the people's delight finds the people delighting in his; a ruler who grieves at the people's grief finds the people grieving at his. To take joy and sorrow together with the whole realm and yet fail to achieve true kingship has never once happened. Mencius then tells a story: Duke Jing of Qi once asked his minister Yanzi how he might cultivate himself to match the touring of the former kings, wishing to travel south along the coast to Langye as they had. Yanzi answered brilliantly: when the Son of Heaven visits the feudal lords it is called "inspecting the guarded lands," meaning he surveys what is under his protection; when the lords appear before the Son of Heaven it is called "reporting on duties," meaning they account for what they have accomplished — neither is idle travel. In spring the ruler inspects the plowing and aids those short of means; in autumn he inspects the harvest and assists those in want. An old Xia proverb says, "If our king does not tour, how shall we rest? If our king does not inspect, how shall we be helped?" — the ruler's touring was meant to be a model for the lords to follow. But now it is otherwise: armies march and consume the grain, the hungry go unfed, the weary go unrested, the people glare sidelong and slander one another, and wickedness takes root; the ruler defies Heaven's mandate, oppresses the people, and squanders food and drink like flowing water, sinking into indulgence — a source of worry to the other lords. Following the current down and forgetting to return is called "drifting"; struggling against it up and forgetting to return is called "clinging"; chasing game without limit is called "wildness"; indulging in drink without limit is called "ruin." The former kings had none of this drifting, clinging, wildness, or ruin — everything depends on what the ruler himself chooses. Duke Jing, delighted at this answer, issued sweeping instructions throughout the state, moved out to lodge in the suburbs, opened the granaries to relieve the needy, and summoned his grand music master, saying, "Compose for me music that lets ruler and minister rejoice together." This became the pieces Zhengzhao and Jiaozhao, whose lyrics ask, "What fault is there in cherishing one's ruler?" — "cherishing the ruler" meaning simply to love him.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `領導的休假與排場若讓基層更苦，快樂會反噬。把「大家是否也能鬆一口氣」納入行程設計。
<br /><b>建議：安排下一次團隊活動或個人休假時，同步做一件減輕他人負擔的事（補人力、補資源或延後非急件）。</b>`,
      en: `A leader's leisure and display that worsen the load below rebound as resentment. Build into plans whether others can also breathe.
<br /><b>Suggestion: When you plan the next team event or personal break, pair it with one act that lightens others' load (cover, resources, or deferring non-urgent work).</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 12,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.5",
    title: { zh: "先斯四者", en: "First the four destitute" },
    text: {
      zh: `齊宣王問曰：「人皆謂我毀明堂，毀諸？已乎？」 

　　孟子對曰：「夫明堂者，王者之堂也。王欲行王政，則勿毀之矣。」 

　　王曰：「王政可得聞與？」 

　　對曰：「昔者文王之治岐也，耕者九一，仕者世祿，關市譏而不征，澤梁無禁，罪人不孥。老而無妻曰鰥，老而無夫曰寡，老而無子曰獨，幼而無父曰孤。此四者，天下之窮民而無告者。文王發政施仁，必先斯四者。詩云，『哿矣富人，哀此煢獨。』」 

　　王曰：「善哉言乎！」 

　　曰：「王如善之，則何為不行？」 

　　王曰：「寡人有疾，寡人好貨。」 

　　對曰：「昔者公劉好貨，《詩》云：『乃積乃倉，乃裹餱糧，於橐於囊。思戢用光。弓矢斯張，干戈戚揚，爰方啟行。』故居者有積倉，行者有裹糧也，然後可以爰方啟行。王如好貨，與百姓同之，於王何有？」 

　　王曰：「寡人有疾，寡人好色。」 

　　對曰：「昔者大王好色，愛厥妃。《詩》云：『古公亶父，來朝走馬；率西水滸，至於岐下；爰及姜女，聿來胥宇。』當是時也，內無怨女，外無曠夫；王如好色，與百姓同之，於王何有？」`,
      en: `King Xuan of Qi asked, "Everyone tells me to destroy the Hall of Light — shall I destroy it, or let it be?"

Mencius replied, "The Hall of Light is the hall of a true king. If Your Majesty wishes to practice kingly government, then do not destroy it."

"May I hear of kingly government?"

"Formerly when King Wen governed Qi, cultivators paid one in nine; those in office had hereditary stipends; at passes and markets there was inspection but no tax; ponds and weirs were without prohibition; and the guilty were not implicated with their wives and children. The old without a wife are called widowers; the old without a husband, widows; the old without children, the solitary; the young without a father, orphans. These four are the poorest of the world's people, with none to whom to appeal. When King Wen issued government and practiced humaneness, he always put these four first. The Poetry says, 'The rich may get by; alas for these lonely and desolate!'"

The king said, "Excellent words!"

"If Your Majesty finds them good, why not practice them?"

"I have a fault: I love goods."

"Formerly Gong Liu loved goods. The Poetry says, 'He piled and stored in granaries; he wrapped provisions in sacks and bags. He thought to gather and make bright. Bows and arrows were set out; shields, dagger-axes, and axes raised; then he began to march.' So those who stayed had stores in granaries, and those who went had wrapped provisions — only then could they begin to march. If Your Majesty loves goods and shares them with the people, what difficulty is there in kingship?"

"I have a fault: I love beauty."

"Formerly King Tai loved beauty and cherished his consort. The Poetry says, 'Ancient Duke Danfu came at dawn, urging his horse; he followed the western riverbank and came to the foot of Mount Qi; and with the Lady Jiang he came to look for a dwelling.' At that time within there were no resentful women, without no unmarried men. If Your Majesty loves beauty and shares it with the people, what difficulty is there in kingship?"`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `齊宣王問：人人都勸我拆毀明堂，究竟該拆還是不拆？孟子答：明堂是行王道之人才有資格使用的殿堂，大王若想推行王政，就不要拆毀它。宣王追問王政的內容，孟子便舉文王治理岐地為例：農民只需繳納九分之一的稅賦，做官的人享有世代承襲的俸祿，關卡市場只稽查不徵稅，湖澤堤梁向百姓開放不加禁止，犯罪者也不牽連妻兒。年老無妻叫做「鰥」，年老無夫叫做「寡」，年老無子叫做「獨」，年幼無父叫做「孤」——這四種人是天下最窮困、又無處申訴的人。文王施行仁政，必定優先照顧這四種人。《詩經》說：「哿矣富人，哀此煢獨」，正是說富人還過得去，最該憐憫的是這些孤苦無依的人。宣王聽了讚歎「說得真好」，孟子便追問：既然覺得好，為何不去實行呢？宣王這才坦言自己有個毛病——喜好財貨。孟子並不責怪，反舉公劉為例：公劉同樣喜好財貨，《詩經》記載他積糧入倉、包裹乾糧裝入橐囊，一心想著安頓百姓、光大家業，弓箭齊備、干戈整肅，這才動身遷徙——正因為留守的人有存糧、遠行的人有乾糧，才能順利出發。大王若喜好財貨，只要能與百姓共享，這對成就王業又有什麼妨礙呢？宣王又坦言自己還有個毛病——喜好女色。孟子仍舉先例寬慰他：太王同樣喜好女色、鍾愛他的妃子姜女，《詩經》記載太王清晨騎馬出發，沿著西方水濱，來到岐山之下，帶著姜女一同尋找安居之地。那個時代，宮內沒有怨恨的女子，宮外也沒有找不到配偶的男子。大王若喜好女色，只要能與百姓共享安居配偶的福分，這對成就王業又有什麼妨礙呢？`,
      en: `King Xuan of Qi asks: everyone urges me to tear down the Hall of Light — should I tear it down, or leave it standing? Mencius answers that the Hall of Light belongs to one who practices true kingly rule, so if the king intends to practice kingly government, he should not destroy it. When the king asks what kingly government entails, Mencius cites King Wen's rule of Qi: farmers paid only one part in nine, officials received hereditary stipends, passes and markets were inspected but not taxed, marshes and weirs were open to all without prohibition, and the guilty were not punished together with their wives and children. The old without a wife are called widowers, the old without a husband widows, the old without children the solitary, and the young without a father orphans — these four are the poorest people under Heaven, with nowhere to turn. When King Wen practiced humane government, he always looked after these four first. The Poetry says, "The rich may get by; pity these lonely and desolate ones" — meaning the wealthy can manage on their own, while these forsaken people deserve compassion most of all. The king praises this as excellent, and Mencius presses him: if you find it good, why not put it into practice? Only then does the king admit his fault — a love of wealth. Mencius, far from reproaching him, cites Gong Liu, who also loved wealth: the Poetry describes him storing grain in granaries, wrapping provisions into sacks and bundles, intent on settling his people and glorifying his house, readying bows and weapons before finally setting out — and it was precisely because those who stayed had stores and those who traveled had provisions that the migration could succeed. If the king loves wealth but shares it with the people, what obstacle is that to true kingship? The king then confesses another fault — a love of beauty. Mencius again reassures him with precedent: King Tai likewise loved beauty and cherished his consort, Lady Jiang; the Poetry describes him riding out at dawn, following the western riverbank to the foot of Mount Qi, bringing Lady Jiang along to find a place to settle. In that era, there were no resentful unmarried women within the palace nor unmarried men without. If the king loves beauty but ensures the people too can enjoy settled homes and spouses, what obstacle is that to true kingship?`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `政策與善心若忽略最無告的人，就不完整。個人嗜好也可以轉成與人同享的安排，而不必先自責到不能動。
<br /><b>建議：本週主動幫助一位較孤立無援的人（長者、單親、新來者），並把一項你的喜好改成可與人共享的形式。</b>`,
      en: `Policy and goodwill are incomplete if they skip those with no one to appeal to. Personal tastes can become shared arrangements without first shaming yourself into paralysis.
<br /><b>Suggestion: This week help one relatively isolated person (elder, single parent, newcomer), and reshape one preference of yours into a form others can share.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 13,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.6",
    title: { zh: "顧左右而言他", en: "Looking left and right, he changed the subject" },
    text: {
      zh: `孟子謂齊宣王，曰：「王之臣有托其妻子於其友而之楚游者，比其反也，則凍餒其妻子，則如之何？」 

　　王曰：「棄之。」 

　　曰：「士師不能治士，則如之何？」 

　　王曰：「已之。」 

　　曰：「四境之內不治，則如之何？」 

　　王顧左右而言他。`,
      en: `Mencius said to King Xuan of Qi, "Suppose a subject of Your Majesty entrusted his wife and children to a friend and traveled to Chu, and when he returned found them freezing and starving — what then?"

The king said, "Cast him off."

"If the chief criminal judge cannot govern his officers — what then?"

"Remove him."

"If within the four borders things are not governed — what then?"

The king looked left and right and spoke of other things.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `孟子層層追問齊宣王的責任感：假如王的臣子把妻子兒女託付給朋友，自己去楚國遊歷，回來卻發現妻子兒女挨凍受餓，該怎麼辦？王說：拋棄這個朋友。士師管不好他手下的士，該怎麼辦？王說：撤他的職。那麼，四境之內治理不好，又該怎麼辦？責任已經逼到國君自己頭上，宣王無言以對，只好左右張望、顧左右而言他。責人容易、責己極難，在這層層逼問裡暴露無遺。`,
      en: `Mencius climbs a ladder of responsibility with King Xuan: if a subject entrusted wife and children to a friend, traveled to Chu, and came home to find them freezing and starving — what then? Cast the friend off. If the chief judge cannot govern his officers — what then? Remove him. If within the four borders things are not governed — what then? The charge has reached the ruler himself; King Xuan cannot answer and looks left and right, changing the subject. Easy to blame others, hard to face oneself — the ladder lays it bare.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `追責時若層層指向別人，最後一層指向自己時最怕逃避。練習把最後一問留給自己的職分。
<br /><b>建議：今天對一件出問題的事，寫下三層責任（執行者、管理者、你自己），並就「你自己」那層採取一項補救。</b>`,
      en: `When blame climbs the ladder, the hardest rung is your own. Practice saving the last question for your role.
<br /><b>Suggestion: For one failure today, write three layers of responsibility (doer, manager, yourself) and take one remedy on your own layer.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 14,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.7",
    title: { zh: "國人皆曰賢", en: "When the people of the state all say he is worthy" },
    text: {
      zh: `孟子見齊宣王，曰：「所謂故國者，非謂有喬木之謂也，有世臣之謂也。王無親臣矣，昔者所進，今日不知其亡也。」 

　　王曰：「吾何以識其不才而捨之？」 

　　曰：「國君進賢，如不得已，將使卑逾尊，疏逾戚，可不慎與？左右皆曰賢，未可也；諸大夫皆曰賢，未可也；國人皆曰賢，然後察之；見賢焉，然後用之。左右皆曰不可，勿聽；諸大夫皆曰不可，勿聽；國人皆曰不可，然後察之；見不可焉，然後去之。左右皆曰可殺，勿聽；諸大夫皆曰可殺，勿聽；國人皆曰可殺，然後察之，見可殺焉，然後殺之。故曰，國人殺之也。如此，然後可以為民父母。」`,
      en: `Mencius went to see King Xuan of Qi and said, "What is called an old state is not a matter of tall trees, but of ministers whose families have served for generations. Your Majesty has no close ministers; those you advanced yesterday, today you do not know they are gone."

The king said, "How am I to recognize the untalented and set them aside?"

Mencius said, "When a ruler of a state advances the worthy, as if he cannot help it, he will make the low overstep the high, the distant overstep the near — can he but be careful? When those at his left and right all say someone is worthy, it is not yet enough; when the great officers all say he is worthy, it is not yet enough; when the people of the state all say he is worthy, then examine him; seeing that he is worthy, then employ him. When left and right all say he will not do, do not listen; when the great officers all say he will not do, do not listen; when the people of the state all say he will not do, then examine him; seeing that he will not do, then remove him. When left and right all say he may be killed, do not listen; when the great officers all say he may be killed, do not listen; when the people of the state all say he may be killed, then examine him; seeing that he may be killed, then kill him. Therefore it is said, The people of the state killed him. Only thus can one be father and mother to the people."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `孟子拜見齊宣王，說：所謂歷史悠久的故國，並不是指國內有多少年代久遠的高大喬木，而是指有多少世代承襲、值得信賴的老臣。如今大王身邊已經沒有可以親近信賴的老臣了，過去所任用提拔的人，如今離開了，大王竟也毫不知情。宣王問：那我要怎樣才能在起用之前就識別出這人是庸才、把他捨棄不用呢？孟子答：一國之君要進用賢才，若真是出於不得已，有時甚至會讓地位低的人越過地位高的人、關係疏遠的人越過關係親近的人，這樣的事怎能不謹慎呢？左右親信都說某人賢能，還不能就此採信；眾位大夫都說某人賢能，也還不能就此採信；等到全國百姓都說某人賢能，這才應當去仔細考察；親自見到他確實賢能，然後才任用他。左右親信都說某人不行，不要輕信；眾位大夫都說某人不行，也不要輕信；等到全國百姓都說某人不行，這才應當去仔細考察；親自見到他確實不行，然後才罷黜他。左右親信都說某人該殺，不要輕信；眾位大夫都說某人該殺，也不要輕信；等到全國百姓都說某人該殺，這才應當去仔細考察；親自見到他確實該殺，然後才處死他。所以說，這是全國百姓把他處死的，而不是君主一人獨斷。能做到這樣，才可以稱得上是百姓的父母官。`,
      en: `Mencius, seeing King Xuan of Qi, explains that a state's antiquity is measured not by how many ancient trees grow within its borders, but by how many ministers of long-standing, trustworthy service it retains. The king, he notes, no longer has any close, trusted ministers at all — those he once promoted have since departed, and the king was not even aware of it. The king asks how he might identify the untalented and set them aside before ever employing them. Mencius answers that a ruler advancing the worthy, if truly compelled by necessity, may sometimes have to let the lowly surpass the exalted and the distant surpass the close — a matter demanding real caution. When those closest to the ruler call someone worthy, that alone is not enough to believe; when all the officials call someone worthy, that too is not enough; only when the entire state calls someone worthy should the ruler look into it himself, and only once he personally observes their worth should he employ them. The same care applies to removing the unfit: the opinion of courtiers or officials alone does not suffice — only the judgment of the whole state, confirmed by personal observation, justifies dismissal. And the same holds even for a capital sentence: not courtiers, not officials, but the whole state's judgment, verified in person, before anyone is put to death. Hence it is said that it is the people of the state themselves who put such a person to death, not the ruler acting alone. Only a ruler who governs this way can truly be called father and mother to the people.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `用人若只聽小圈子，容易誤判。把「多方聽取＋親自核實」做成習慣，尤其在拔擢與淘汰時。
<br /><b>建議：對一位你準備重用或疏遠的人，額外問兩位立場不同的人，再做一次具體事實核對後再決定。</b>`,
      en: `Hiring by an inner circle alone invites error. Make wide hearing plus personal verification a habit, especially for promotion and removal.
<br /><b>Suggestion: For someone you mean to elevate or sideline, ask two people with different standpoints, verify one concrete fact, then decide.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 15,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.8",
    title: { zh: "誅一夫紂", en: "Punishing the outcast Zhou" },
    text: {
      zh: `齊宣王問曰：「湯放桀，武王伐紂，有諸？」 

　　孟子對曰：「於傳有之。」 

　　曰：「臣弒其君可乎？」 

　　曰：「賊仁者謂之『賊』，賊義者謂之『殘』。殘賊之人謂之『一夫』。聞誅一夫紂矣，未聞弒君也。」`,
      en: `King Xuan of Qi asked, "Tang banished Jie, and King Wu attacked Zhou — was that so?"

Mencius replied, "It is so in the records."

"May a subject murder his ruler?"

"One who injures humaneness is called a robber; one who injures rightness is called a ruffian. A robber and ruffian is called a mere fellow. I have heard of the punishment of the fellow Zhou; I have not heard of the murder of a ruler."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `齊宣王問起史傳中湯放逐夏桀、武王討伐商紂是否屬實，孟子說記載上確有其事。宣王接著逼問：那豈不是臣子弒君嗎？孟子重新為名分定性：賊害仁的叫做「賊」，賊害義的叫做「殘」；殘賊之人只配稱「一夫」——獨夫。他所聽說的是誅殺了一個叫紂的獨夫，從未聽說過那是弒君。政權是否仍叫「君」，要看是否還合於仁義，不能只靠空名位來嚇阻討伐。`,
      en: `King Xuan of Qi asks whether the records are right that Tang banished Jie and King Wu attacked Zhou Xin; Mencius says they are. The king presses: then may a subject murder his ruler? Mencius redefines the names: one who injures humaneness is a "robber," one who injures rightness a "ruffian"; a robber-ruffian is only a "lone fellow" — a mere tyrant. What he has heard is the punishment of the fellow Zhou, not the murder of a ruler. Whether a throne still counts as "ruler" tracks humaneness and rightness, not an empty title used to forbid rightful chastisement.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `名位不能自動合理化傷害。對權威的服從，仍要以是否殘害基本道義為界。
<br /><b>建議：遇到一項「上級／慣例如此」卻明顯傷人的要求，今天用一句話清楚拒絕或提出更合乎義的替代方案。</b>`,
      en: `Rank does not automatically justify harm. Obedience to authority still has a boundary where basic rightness is ruined.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Facing one 'orders / custom say so' demand that clearly wounds people, refuse it today in one clear sentence or offer a more righteous alternative.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 16,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.9",
    title: { zh: "勿教玉人琢玉", en: "Do not teach the jade-carver his craft" },
    text: {
      zh: `孟子見齊宣王，曰：「為巨室，則必使工師求大木。工師得大木，則王喜，以為能勝其任也。匠人斫而小之，則王怒，以為不勝其任矣。夫人幼而學之，壯而欲行之，王曰：『姑捨女所學而從我』，則何如？今有璞玉於此，雖萬鎰，必使玉人雕琢之。至於治國家，則曰：『姑捨女所學而從我』，則何以異於教玉人雕琢玉哉？」`,
      en: `Mencius went to see King Xuan of Qi and said, "When building a great house, you must have the master of works seek great timber. When he finds great timber, Your Majesty is pleased, thinking him equal to his charge. If the artisans chop it small, Your Majesty is angry, thinking them unequal to their charge. Now a person studies something in youth and wishes to practice it in maturity; if Your Majesty says, 'For now set aside what you have learned and follow me' — what of that? Suppose here there is uncut jade, worth even ten thousand yi; you would certainly have a jade-worker carve it. But when it comes to governing the state, you say, 'For now set aside what you have learned and follow me' — how does that differ from teaching a jade-worker how to carve jade?"`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `孟子對齊宣王說：要建造大房子，必定派工師去尋找大木材；工師找到大木材，大王便高興，認為他勝任這份差事；若木匠把大木材砍削得太小，大王便會發怒，認為他不能勝任。一個人從小學習某項本領，長大後便想要施展它；如果大王卻對他說「暫且放下你所學的，聽從我的意思去做」，這會是什麼結果？如今若有一塊未經雕琢的璞玉在此，即使價值萬鎰之多，也必定要請玉匠來雕琢它；可是一到治理國家大事，大王卻說「暫且放下你所學的，聽從我」，這與教玉匠該怎麼雕玉，又有什麼分別呢？`,
      en: `Mencius says to King Xuan of Qi: to build a great house, one must send the master builder to find great timber; when he finds it, the king is pleased, thinking him equal to the task, but if the carpenters chop the wood down too small, the king grows angry, thinking them unequal to it. A person studies a skill from youth and, on reaching maturity, wishes to put it into practice; if the king then tells him, "For now set aside what you have learned and simply follow my wishes," what would come of that? Suppose there is here a piece of uncarved jade, worth even ten thousand yi in gold — one would certainly hire a jade-carver to work it. Yet when it comes to governing the state, the king says, "For now set aside what you have learned and follow me" — how does this differ from personally instructing the jade-carver how he ought to carve his jade?`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `外行領導若強改專業判斷，常把材料砍小、把玉毀壞。授權前先問自己是否在「教玉人琢玉」。
<br /><b>建議：今天在一項你不專精的事上，改成先請教負責者的專業意見，並至少採納其中一點具體建議。</b>`,
      en: `Amateur command that overrides trained judgment often chops timber small and ruins jade. Before directing, ask whether you are 'teaching the carver.'
<br /><b>Suggestion: On one matter outside your expertise today, ask the responsible person first and adopt at least one concrete recommendation.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 17,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.10",
    title: { zh: "民悅則取", en: "Take it if the people rejoice" },
    text: {
      zh: `齊人伐燕，勝之。宣王問曰：「或謂寡人勿取，或謂寡人取之。以萬乘之國伐萬乘之國，五旬而舉之，人力不至於此。不取必有天殃。取之何如？」 

　　孟子對曰：「取之而燕民悅，則取之。古之人有行之者，武王是也。取之而燕民不悅，則勿取，古之人有行之者，文王是也。以萬乘之國伐萬乘之國，簞食壺漿以迎王師，豈有他哉？避水火也。如水益深，如火益熱，亦運而已矣。」`,
      en: `The people of Qi attacked Yan and defeated it. King Xuan asked, "Some tell me not to take it; some tell me to take it. With a state of ten thousand chariots attacking a state of ten thousand chariots, to take it in fifty days is beyond human strength. If I do not take it, there will surely be Heaven's calamity. What of taking it?"

Mencius replied, "If you take it and the people of Yan rejoice, then take it. Among the ancients there was one who did so — King Wu. If you take it and the people of Yan do not rejoice, then do not take it; among the ancients there was one who acted so — King Wen. When a state of ten thousand chariots attacks another, and people meet the royal army with baskets of food and jugs of drink — is there any other reason? They are fleeing water and fire. If the water grows deeper and the fire hotter, they will simply turn elsewhere."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `齊國攻打燕國並且獲勝之後，宣王問孟子：有人勸我不要吞併燕國，有人勸我吞併它。以擁有萬輛兵車的大國去攻打同樣擁有萬輛兵車的大國，五十天就攻下了，這已超出人力所能做到的地步；若不趁機拿下，恐怕會招致上天的災殃。究竟該不該吞併它呢？孟子回答：吞併之後若燕國百姓感到喜悅，那就吞併它——古人之中有這麼做的，武王便是；吞併之後若燕國百姓不感到喜悅，那就不要吞併它——古人之中也有這麼做的，文王便是。以擁有萬輛兵車的大國去攻打另一個萬輛兵車的大國，百姓卻用竹籃盛飯、用壺盛水漿來迎接大王的軍隊，難道有別的原因嗎？不過是想擺脫像水深火熱一般的苦難處境罷了。如果吞併之後，水反而更深、火反而更熱，百姓不過是轉而投向別處尋求解脫罷了。`,
      en: `After Qi defeats Yan, King Xuan asks Mencius: some advise me not to annex it, others urge me to. With a state of ten thousand chariots attacking another of ten thousand chariots and taking it in fifty days, this exceeds what human strength alone could accomplish; if I fail to seize the moment, Heaven may send calamity upon me. Should I annex it or not? Mencius answers: if, once annexed, the people of Yan rejoice, then annex it — among the ancients there were those who did so, King Wu being one; if, once annexed, the people of Yan do not rejoice, then do not annex it — among the ancients there were also those who acted this way, King Wen being one. When a state of ten thousand chariots attacks another state of ten thousand chariots, and the people greet the king's army with baskets of food and jars of drink, is there any other reason for it than that they are trying to escape a situation as unbearable as deep water and scorching fire? If, after annexation, the water only grows deeper and the fire only grows hotter, the people will simply turn elsewhere in search of relief.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `機會來時別只問「天時是否允許」，更要問「當事人是否得救」。擴張若加重苦難，名分再好也站不住。
<br /><b>建議：面對一項「難得的擴張／併入」機會，先直接聽取受影響者的感受，再決定進退。</b>`,
      en: `When chance comes, ask not only whether Heaven seems to allow it, but whether the people concerned are rescued. Expansion that worsens suffering cannot stand on a fine name.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Facing one rare chance to expand or absorb, hear those affected first, then decide to advance or hold back.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 18,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.11",
    title: { zh: "置君而去", en: "Install a ruler and withdraw" },
    text: {
      zh: `齊人伐燕，取之。諸侯將謀救燕。宣王曰：「諸侯多謀伐寡人者，何以待之？」 

　　孟子對曰：「臣聞七十里為政於天下者，湯是也。未聞以千里畏人者也。書曰：『湯一征，自葛始。』天下信之，東面而征，西夷怨；南面而征，北狄怨曰：『奚為後我？』民望之，若大旱之望雲霓也。歸市者不止，耕者不變。誅其君而弔其民，若時雨降。民大悅。書曰：『徯我后，后來其蘇。』今燕虐其民，王往而征之，民以為將拯己於水火之中也，簞食壺漿以迎王師。若殺其兄父，係累其子弟，毀其宗廟，遷其重器，如之何其可也？天下固畏齊之強也，今又倍地而不行仁政，是動天下之兵也。王速出令，反其旄倪，止其重器，謀於燕眾，置君而後去之，則猶可及止也。」`,
      en: `The people of Qi attacked Yan and took it. The feudal lords were about to plot to rescue Yan. King Xuan said, "Many lords plot to attack me — how shall I meet them?"

Mencius replied, "I have heard of one who with seventy li administered government for the world — Tang. I have not heard of one who with a thousand li stood in fear of others. The Documents say, 'Tang's first campaign began with Ge.' The world trusted him; when he campaigned eastward, the western tribes complained; when southward, the northern Di complained, saying, 'Why put us last?' The people looked for him as in great drought one looks for clouds and rainbows. Those returning to market did not stop; plowmen did not change. He punished their rulers and comforted their people, like timely rain. The people were greatly pleased. The Documents say, 'Wait for our lord; when the lord comes, we shall revive.' Now Yan oppressed its people; Your Majesty went and punished it, and the people thought you would rescue them from water and fire, meeting your army with baskets of food and jugs of drink. If you kill their fathers and elder brothers, bind their sons and younger brothers, destroy their ancestral temples, and move their precious vessels — how can that be right? The world already feared Qi's strength; now you double your territory and do not practice humane government — this is to stir the world's armies. Quickly issue orders: return the old and the young you have taken, stop moving the precious vessels, consult the multitude of Yan, install a ruler, and then withdraw — then you may still stop it in time."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `齊國攻取燕國之後，各國諸侯開始謀劃聯合出兵救援燕國。宣王問孟子：許多諸侯都在謀劃攻打我，該如何應付？孟子回答：我只聽說過湯憑藉七十里之地便能為天下行王道，卻沒聽說過誰擁有千里之地反而畏懼他人。《尚書》記載湯的第一次征伐從葛國開始，天下人都信任他，他向東方征伐，西方的夷人便埋怨；向南方征伐，北方的狄人便埋怨，說「為什麼把我們放在後面」。百姓盼望他，就像大旱之年盼望雲霓一樣殷切；集市照常往來，農夫照常耕作，湯只誅殺暴虐的君主、撫慰受苦的百姓，如同及時雨降臨一般，百姓因此大為歡喜。《尚書》說：「等待我們的君王，君王一來，我們就能重獲新生」，正是這個意思。如今燕國的君主虐待自己的百姓，大王前往征討，百姓原以為大王將把自己從水深火熱之中拯救出來，才用竹籃盛飯、壺盛水漿來迎接大王的軍隊。可是如果大王卻殺害他們的父兄、囚禁他們的子弟、毀壞他們的宗廟、搬走他們的傳國重器，這怎麼可以呢？天下本來就已畏懼齊國的強大，如今又兼併了一倍的土地，卻不施行仁政，這無異於招惹天下的軍隊前來討伐。大王應當趕緊下令：歸還所擄掠的老弱婦孺，停止搬運燕國的重器，與燕國百姓商議，為他們擁立一位新君，然後才撤軍離開——這樣或許還來得及阻止一場大戰。`,
      en: `After Qi conquers Yan, the other feudal lords begin plotting a joint campaign to rescue it. King Xuan asks Mencius how to respond to the many lords scheming against him. Mencius answers: I have only heard of Tang, who with a mere seventy li of land practiced kingly rule over the whole world; I have never heard of anyone with a thousand li of land living in fear of others. The Documents record that Tang's first campaign began with the state of Ge, and the whole world trusted him — when he campaigned eastward, the western tribes complained; when southward, the northern tribes complained, asking why they were left for last. The people longed for him as one longs for clouds and rainbow in a great drought; markets carried on as usual, farmers kept plowing as usual, for Tang punished only the cruel rulers and comforted the suffering people, arriving like timely rain, and the people rejoiced greatly. The Documents say, "We wait for our lord; when the lord comes, we shall be restored to life" — exactly this. Now the ruler of Yan oppressed his own people, and when the king went to punish him, the people believed he would rescue them from a life of deep water and scorching fire, and so greeted his army with baskets of food and jars of drink. But if the king now kills their fathers and elder brothers, imprisons their sons and younger brothers, destroys their ancestral temples, and carts off their state's treasured vessels, how can this be right? The world already feared Qi's strength, and now, having doubled its territory without practicing humane government, Qi only invites the armies of the whole world to march against it. The king should quickly issue orders: return the captured elderly, women, and children; stop removing Yan's treasured vessels; consult with the people of Yan and help them establish a new ruler; and only then withdraw the army — only this may still be in time to prevent a full-scale war.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `勝利之後最危險的是把「拯救」變成「佔有」。及時退讓、交還、共立秩序，才能止住眾怒。
<br /><b>建議：在一項你已佔上風的爭議裡，主動歸還一項多餘所得，並讓對方參與訂出善後規則。</b>`,
      en: `After a win, the danger is turning rescue into possession. Timely return, restraint, and shared order can still stop collective anger.
<br /><b>Suggestion: In one dispute where you already have the upper hand, voluntarily return one surplus gain and let the other side help set the aftermath rules.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 19,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.12",
    title: { zh: "出爾反爾", en: "What goes out from you returns to you" },
    text: {
      zh: `鄒與魯鬨。穆公問曰：「吾有司死者三十三人，而民莫之死也。誅之，則不可勝誅；不誅，則疾視其長上之死而不救，如之何則可也？」 

　　孟子對曰：「凶年饑歲，君之民老弱轉乎溝壑，壯者散而之四方者，幾千人矣；而君之倉廩實，府庫充，有司莫以告，是上慢而殘下也。曾子曰：『戒之戒之！出乎爾者，反乎爾者也。』夫民今而後得反之也。君無尤焉！君行仁政，斯民親其上，死其長矣。」`,
      en: `Zou and Lu clashed. Duke Mu asked, "Of my officers thirty-three died, and none of the people would die for them. If I punish them, I cannot punish them all; if I do not, I look with anger on their watching their superiors die without rescue — what then may be done?"

Mencius replied, "In years of famine and hunger, of Your Lordship's people the old and weak have rolled into the gullies by the thousands, and the able-bodied have scattered to the four quarters — yet your granaries are full and storehouses stuffed, and the officers do not report it. This is superiors being negligent and harming those below. Zengzi said, 'Beware, beware! What goes out from you returns to you.' Now at last the people have a chance to return it. Do not blame them! If Your Lordship practices humane government, then the people will be close to their superiors and die for their leaders."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `鄒國與魯國發生衝突，鄒穆公問孟子：我方的官吏死了三十三人，百姓卻沒有一個為他們赴死救援的。若要治百姓的罪，卻殺不勝殺；若不治罪，又痛恨他們眼看著上級長官戰死卻見死不救，該怎麼辦才好？孟子回答：遇到荒年饑歲，君王的百姓中，年老體弱的輾轉死在溝壑之中，年輕力壯的四散逃往各地，這樣的人有數千之多；然而君王的糧倉裡糧食充實、府庫裡財貨充盈，官吏們卻沒有一個把這件事稟告君王的，這就是在上位者怠慢失職、殘害百姓的表現。曾子說過：「要警惕啊，要警惕啊！你怎樣對待別人，別人也會怎樣對待你。」百姓如今，正是得到了一個回報的機會而已，君王不必怨恨他們！只要君王能夠施行仁政，百姓自然會親近他們的上級、願意為長官效死。`,
      en: `When conflict breaks out between Zou and Lu, Duke Mu of Zou asks Mencius: thirty-three of my officers died, yet not a single one of the people would die to save them. If I punish the people, I cannot punish them all; if I do not, I resent their watching their superiors die in battle without coming to the rescue — what should I do? Mencius answers: in years of famine and hunger, the old and weak among the king's own people have died one by one in ditches and gullies, while the able-bodied have scattered in flight to every direction, numbering in the thousands — and yet the king's granaries remained full of grain and the treasuries full of wealth, with not a single officer reporting this to him. This is exactly what it looks like when those above grow negligent and, through that neglect, harm those below. Zengzi once said: "Be on guard, be on guard! However you treat others, they will treat you the same way in return." The people have simply now found an occasion to return what was done to them — the king need not resent them for it! If the king can only put humane government into practice, the people will naturally draw close to their superiors and be willing to die for their leaders.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `平時不體恤，危時莫怪無人效死。你怎樣待人，人就怎樣回報。先補「上慢殘下」的缺口。
<br /><b>建議：找出一處你（或你的組織）對基層長期遲緩回應的事，本週內完成一項實質補救並公開說明。</b>`,
      en: `Neglect in peacetime; do not wonder at no one dying for you in crisis. Coldness returns in kind. First repair where superiors have harmed those below.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Find one place you (or your group) have long been slow to answer people at the base; finish one real remedy this week and say so openly.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 20,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.13",
    title: { zh: "與民死守", en: "Hold the walls with the people" },
    text: {
      zh: `滕文公問曰：「滕，小國也，間於齊、楚。事齊乎？事楚乎？」 

　　孟子對曰：「是謀非吾所能及也。無已，則有一焉：鑿斯池也，築斯城也，與民守之，效死而民弗去，則是可為也。」`,
      en: `Duke Wen of Teng asked, "Teng is a small state, between Qi and Chu. Shall I serve Qi, or serve Chu?"

Mencius replied, "This plan is not something I can reach. If there is no alternative, then there is one thing: dig these moats, build these walls, and hold them with the people; be ready to die, and if the people will not leave — then that can be done."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `滕文公問：滕是小國，夾在齊、楚兩大之間，究竟該事齊還是事楚？孟子坦白說，選邊站這種謀算不是他能替人拍板的；若一定要說還有一條路，那就只有：把護城河挖深、城牆築牢，和百姓一起死守；若到了效死的關頭，百姓仍不肯離去——那才真有可為。小國在強鄰之間，根本不在找對靠山，而在是否得民心、能否與民共守。`,
      en: `Duke Wen of Teng asks: Teng is small, squeezed between Qi and Chu — should he serve Qi or Chu? Mencius admits that picking a side is not a calculation he can settle for another; if there must be one path left, it is this: dig the moats deep, build the walls strong, and hold them with the people unto death — and if, when ready to die, the people still will not leave, then that truly can be done. For a small state between strong neighbors, the root is not finding the right patron, but winning the people's heart and holding the ground together.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `夾在強勢之間時，盲從選邊不如先鞏固內部信任。沒有願意留下的人，任何靠山都不穩。
<br /><b>建議：面對兩難選邊時，先做一件強化「自己人願意留下」的事（溝通承諾、補資源、共定底線）。</b>`,
      en: `Caught between powers, blind side-picking beats less than consolidating internal trust. Without people willing to stay, no patron is stable.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Facing a hard choice of sides, first do one thing that strengthens whether 'our people' want to stay (clear promises, resources, a shared bottom line).</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 21,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.14",
    title: { zh: "強為善而已", en: "Simply strive to do good" },
    text: {
      zh: `滕文公問曰：「齊人將築薛，吾甚恐，如之何則可？」 

　　孟子對曰：「昔者大王居邠，狄人侵之，去之岐山之下居焉。非擇而取之，不得已也。苟為善，後世子孫必有王者矣。君子創業垂統，為可繼也。若夫成功，則天也。君如彼何哉？強為善而已矣。」`,
      en: `Duke Wen of Teng asked, "The people of Qi are about to fortify Xue; I am greatly afraid — what may be done?"

Mencius replied, "Formerly King Tai dwelt in Bin; when the Di invaded, he left and dwelt at the foot of Mount Qi. It was not that he chose and took it; he could not do otherwise. If one does good, later generations of descendants will surely have one who becomes a true king. The noble person founds an enterprise and bequeaths a line that can be continued. As for success, that is Heaven's. What can Your Lordship do about them? Simply strive hard to do good — that is all."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `齊人將築薛，滕文公甚恐，問如何是好。孟子舉太王居邠、狄人侵之、去之岐山：非擇而取，不得已也。苟為善，後世子孫必有王者。君子創業垂統，為可繼也；若夫成功，則天也。君如彼何哉？強為善而已矣。強鄰壓境時，奇謀選邊都不是孟子所許的根本；根本是把善政做穩、把可傳的統緒留下，成敗則付之於天——小國自處之道在此。換言之，滕所能操之在己者唯「強為善」與「創業垂統為可繼」；齊築薛之威脅屬彼，成敗之數屬天。孟子不替小國選邊站隊，只把力氣收回來用在可傳的善政上。`,
      en: `Qi will fortify Xue; Duke Wen of Teng is greatly afraid. Mencius cites King Tai leaving Bin for Mount Qi under Di pressure — not a free choice of prize land, but necessity. If one does good, later descendants will surely have a true king. The junzi founds a continuable line; success is Heaven's. What can you do about them? Simply strive hard to do good. Under a strong neighbor, clever side-picking is not the root Mencius allows; the root is steady good government and a bequeathable line, leaving success to Heaven — that is how a small state stands. In short, what Teng can control is only striving to do good and founding a continuable line; Qi's fortifying Xue is theirs, success Heaven's. Mencius will not pick a side for a small state; he pulls effort back onto bequeathable good government.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `外部威脅無法全控時，仍可控自己是否持續行善、留下可繼的規範與信任。
<br /><b>建議：在一件令你焦慮的外部變數旁，列出一項完全由你決定的「為善」行動，並在三天內完成。</b>`,
      en: `When outside threats cannot be fully controlled, you can still control whether you keep doing good and leave continuable norms and trust.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Beside one anxious external variable, name one 'do good' action wholly in your control and finish it within three days.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 22,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.15",
    title: { zh: "事大不得免", en: "Serving the great still brings no escape" },
    text: {
      zh: `滕文公問曰：「滕，小國也；竭力以事大國，則不得免焉，如之何則可？」 

　　孟子對曰：「昔者大王居邠，狄人侵之。事之以皮幣，不得免焉；事之以犬馬，不得免焉；事之以珠玉，不得免焉。乃屬其耆老而告之曰：『狄人之所欲者，吾土地也。吾聞之也：君子不以其所以養人者害人。二三子何患乎無君？我將去之。』去邠，逾梁山，邑於岐山之下居焉。邠人曰：『仁人也，不可失也。』從之者如歸市。或曰：『世守也，非身之所能為也。效死勿去。』君請擇於斯二者。」`,
      en: `Duke Wen of Teng asked, "Teng is a small state; though I exhaust my strength serving the great states, I cannot escape — what then may be done?"

Mencius replied, "Formerly King Tai dwelt in Bin; the Di invaded. He served them with skins and silks, and could not escape; with dogs and horses, and could not escape; with pearls and jade, and could not escape. Then he gathered the elders and told them, 'What the Di want is our land. I have heard: the noble person does not harm people with that by which he nourishes them. Why should you two or three gentlemen worry about having no ruler? I am about to leave.' He left Bin, crossed Mount Liang, and made a settlement at the foot of Mount Qi. The people of Bin said, 'He is a humane man; we must not lose him,' and those who followed him were like people returning to market. Some say, 'This is what we hold for the generations; it is not something one person can do alone. Be ready to die and do not leave.' Let Your Lordship choose between these two."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `滕文公問：滕國是個小國，即使竭盡全力事奉大國，也還是免不了受欺凌，該怎麼辦才好？孟子舉太王的例子回答：從前太王住在邠地，狄人前來侵犯，太王用皮革布帛去侍奉他們，還是免不了侵犯；用狗馬去侍奉他們，還是免不了侵犯；用珠玉去侍奉他們，仍然免不了侵犯。於是太王召集邠地的父老，對他們說：「狄人所想要的，是我們的土地。我聽說過，君子不會為了用來養人的土地反而去害人。你們何必擔心沒有君主呢？我打算離開這裡。」於是太王離開邠地，翻越梁山，在岐山之下建立新的城邑定居下來。邠地的人說：「這是位仁德之人，不可以失去他」，於是跟隨他遷徙的人多得像趕集一般絡繹不絕。但也有人主張：「這是世代相守的基業，不是一個人能夠決定放棄的，應當拚死守護、絕不離開。」孟子最後把選擇權交還給滕文公：這兩條路，請君自己在其中選擇。`,
      en: `Duke Wen of Teng asks: Teng is a small state; even exhausting every effort to serve the great states, we still cannot avoid being oppressed — what should we do? Mencius answers with the example of King Tai: long ago, King Tai dwelt in Bin, and when the Di people invaded, he served them with leather and silk, yet the invasion did not stop; he served them with dogs and horses, yet it did not stop; he served them with pearls and jade, yet it still did not stop. So King Tai gathered the elders of Bin and told them: "What the Di want is our land. I have heard that a noble person does not use the very land meant to nourish his people as a means of harming them. Why should you worry about having no ruler? I intend to leave this place." King Tai then left Bin, crossed Mount Liang, and built a new settlement at the foot of Mount Qi, making his home there. The people of Bin said, "He is a humane man; we must not lose him," and so many followed him in migration that it looked like a crowd rushing to market. Yet others hold a different view: this is an inheritance handed down through generations, not something one person alone has the right to abandon — one should defend it to the death and never leave. In the end, Mencius returns the choice to Duke Wen himself: between these two paths, let the ruler choose for himself.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `無止境的妥協若仍換不來安全，就要正視結構性選擇：保全人，或死守某種不可讓的持守。逃避選擇才最耗損。
<br /><b>建議：寫下你正面臨的一項「事大仍不安」的處境，清楚標出兩個可敬選項的代價，並與信任的人討論後選定方向。</b>`,
      en: `If endless concession still buys no safety, face the structural choice: preserve people, or hold an unyielding charge. Avoiding the choice costs most.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Write one 'serving the great yet unsafe' situation, name the costs of two honorable options, discuss with someone you trust, and choose a direction.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 23,
    label: "梁惠王下 2.16",
    title: { zh: "不遇天也", en: "Not meeting is Heaven's doing" },
    text: {
      zh: `魯平公將出，嬖人臧倉者請曰：「他日君出，則必命有司所之。今乘輿已駕矣，有司未知所之，敢請。」 

　　公曰：「將見孟子。」 

　　曰：「何哉，君所為輕身以先於匹夫者？以為賢乎？禮義由賢者出；而孟子之後喪逾前喪。君無見焉！」 

　　公曰：「諾。」 

　　樂正子入見，曰：「君奚為不見孟軻也？」 

　　曰：「或告寡人曰，『孟子之後喪逾前喪』，是以不往見也。」 

　　曰：「何哉，君所謂逾者？前以士，後以大夫；前以三鼎，而後以五鼎與？」 

　　曰：「否，謂棺槨衣衾之美也。」 

　　曰：「非所謂逾也，貧富不同也。」 

　　樂正子見孟子，曰：「克告於君，君為來見也。嬖人有臧倉者沮君，君是以不果來也。」 

　　曰：「行或使之；止或尼之。行、止，非人所能也。吾之不遇魯侯，天也。臧氏之子，焉能使予不遇哉？」`,
      en: `Duke Ping of Lu was about to go out. His favorite, Zang Cang, requested, "On other days when you go out, you always order the officers where you are going. Now the carriage is already harnessed, and the officers do not know where — may I ask?"

The duke said, "I am going to see Mencius."

"Why would you demean yourself to put a common fellow first? Do you think him worthy? Ritual and rightness come forth from the worthy; yet Mencius's later funeral exceeded his earlier one. Do not see him!"

The duke said, "Agreed."

Yuezhengzi entered and saw him: "Why does Your Lordship not see Meng Ke?"

"Someone told me, 'Mencius's later funeral exceeded his earlier one,' so I did not go to see him."

"What do you mean by 'exceeded'? That the earlier used a scholar's rites and the later a great officer's — three ding before and five after?"

"No — I mean the beauty of the inner and outer coffins and the grave clothes."

"That is not what is meant by exceeding; it is that poverty and wealth differed."

Yuezhengzi saw Mencius and said, "I told the lord, and he was going to come see you. A favorite, Zang Cang, stopped him, so he did not in the end come."

Mencius said, "Going forward — something may impel it; stopping — something may hinder it. Going and stopping are not within human power alone. That I did not meet the Marquis of Lu is Heaven. How could the son of the Zang clan make me fail to meet him?"`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `魯平公準備出門，寵臣臧倉問道：往常您出門，一定會吩咐官員去處，如今車馬都已備好，官員卻不知道要去哪裡，敢問要去何處？平公答：打算去見孟子。臧倉便說：您這樣降低身分、率先去拜訪一個平民百姓，是為什麼呢？難道認為他是賢者嗎？禮義本該由賢者率先做出表率，可是孟子後來安葬母親的規格，卻超過先前安葬父親的規格，這樣的人您不該去見他！平公便說：好，那就不去了。樂正子入宮進見，問道：您為什麼不去見孟軻呢？平公答：有人告訴我「孟子後來的喪禮超過先前的喪禮」，所以我沒有去見他。樂正子追問：您所說的「超過」，指的是什麼？是說先前用士人的禮制、後來卻用大夫的禮制？先前用三鼎、後來卻用五鼎嗎？平公說：不是，我是說棺槨衣衾的精美程度。樂正子解釋道：這並不是所謂的「超過禮制」，只是因為前後貧富不同罷了（孟子先前安葬父親時家境貧寒，後來安葬母親時家境已經寬裕）。樂正子見到孟子後，稟告說：我曾向國君提起您，國君原本打算前來拜見您，卻被一個名叫臧倉的寵臣阻攔，所以最終沒有成行。孟子聽了，淡然回答：一個人前行，或許有什麼在推動他；一個人止步，或許有什麼在阻攔他。前行或止步，都不是人力所能完全掌控的。我沒能與魯侯相見，是天意如此；臧氏的兒子，又怎麼能真的讓我見不到他呢？`,
      en: `Duke Ping of Lu is about to go out. His favorite, Zang Cang, asks: on other days when you go out, you always tell the officials where you are headed; now the carriage is harnessed, yet the officials do not know your destination — may I ask where you are going? The duke replies that he means to visit Mencius. Zang Cang objects: why would you lower yourself to visit a commoner first? Do you consider him worthy? Ritual and rightness ought to be modeled by the worthy, and yet Mencius's later funeral, for his mother, exceeded his earlier one, for his father — you should not go see such a man! The duke agrees to cancel the visit. Yuezhengzi then enters and asks why the duke will not see Meng Ke. The duke explains that someone told him Mencius's later funeral exceeded the earlier one, which is why he did not go. Yuezhengzi presses further: what do you mean by "exceeded"? Did he use a scholar's rites the first time and a great officer's the second — three ritual vessels before and five after? The duke clarifies that he means the fineness of the coffins and burial garments. Yuezhengzi explains that this is not a matter of exceeding proper ritual at all, but simply that Mencius's family was poor at the earlier funeral and better off by the time of the later one. Yuezhengzi then goes to see Mencius and reports: I spoke of you to the ruler, and he was going to come see you, but his favorite Zang Cang blocked him, so in the end he did not come. Mencius answers calmly: when a person moves forward, something may be driving him on; when he stops, something may be holding him back. Moving forward or stopping is not something entirely within human control. That I failed to meet the Marquis of Lu is simply Heaven's doing — how could the son of the Zang family truly prevent me from meeting him?`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `遇小人讒阻時，既要澄清事實，也不必把全部成敗歸於讒人。把能做的說清楚，其餘交給更大的時勢。
<br /><b>建議：若有一次會面或機會被閒話耽誤，先寫下可澄清的一兩點事實並傳達；同時停止反覆揣測讒者，把心力轉回本分工作。</b>`,
      en: `When a favorite's slander blocks you, clarify facts — but do not pin all success or failure on the slanderer. Do what you can say clearly; leave the rest to a larger season.
<br /><b>Suggestion: If gossip costs you a meeting or chance, write one or two clarifiable facts and convey them; then stop replaying the slanderer and return effort to your proper work.</b>`
    }
  }
];

export default mencius02_liangHuiWangXia;
